# subsystems to DISABLE.

# Wireless (802.11) — DELIBERATELY RE-ENABLED 2026-08-03
#
# Wi-Fi is now ON by choice: the device needs wireless networking. This weakens
# the hardening posture (the 802.11 stack is a large, network-facing attack
# surface) and is a deliberate trade-off, not an oversight.
#
# To turn Wi-Fi back OFF, change the four "##" markers below back to "#".
# The directives are disabled rather than deleted so the hard-won knowledge here
# is not lost — parse_disabled_symbols() in scripts/lib.sh only recognises lines
# starting exactly "# CONFIG_", so "##" makes it skip them.
#
# KEEP THIS NOTE: disabling CONFIG_WIRELESS alone is NOT enough. The Wi-Fi
# *drivers* under CONFIG_WLAN (b43, ath9k, ...) `select CFG80211`, and
# `make olddefconfig` then forces the wireless stack back ON. You must also
# disable the driver umbrella (WLAN) and the stack symbols (CFG80211/MAC80211)
# that get re-selected. With the drivers gone, nothing selects the stack and the
# disables finally stick. This cost a full rebuild to discover.
##  CONFIG_WIRELESS is not set
##  CONFIG_WLAN is not set
##  CONFIG_CFG80211 is not set
##  CONFIG_MAC80211 is not set

# Bluetooth
# CONFIG_BT is not set

# Sound
# CONFIG_SOUND is not set

# Module symbol versioning (MODVERSIONS)
# Not a subsystem — a build-cost and surface reduction. With MODVERSIONS on, the
# kernel build preprocesses every module source a SECOND time
# ("gcc -E -D__GENKSYMS__ ... | genksyms") purely to compute symbol-version CRCs.
# Observed live: every sampled cc1 during a build was one of these passes.
#
# Its only purpose is letting out-of-tree modules be version-checked against this
# kernel — and we dropped the linux-rpi-dev subpackage, so out-of-tree modules
# can't be built against it anyway. We pay the cost for a capability the design
# already forgoes. Expect roughly 15-25% off the kernel build (an -E pass is
# cheaper than a full compile, so this is not a halving).
# CONFIG_MODVERSIONS is not set
